  (commentary generated by claude-fable-5)
Energy is the story this morning: crude is unwinding its geopolitical premium in dramatic fashion after President Trump softened his tone on Iran and, according to reports, postponed a planned strike. Brent is down 4.75% at $83.75 and WTI off 5.11%, with the products complex hit harder still — gasoil collapsed 11.6% and heating oil 5.3% — while options flow shows fresh Brent downside put spreads and falling implied vol, confirming the market is hedging further de-escalation rather than escalation. That fading war premium is the tide lifting everything else: equity-index futures are ripping (S&P +1.45%, Nasdaq +1.72%, Russell +1.75%, Dow at a record), Treasuries are bid across the curve with the long bond up 0.72% as the inflation scare embedded in $90+ oil deflates, and even a four-year-high ISM print of 55.6 couldn't derail the bond rally.

The other live macro thread is FX: the yen jumped 1.4% after Tokyo and Washington confirmed joint intervention and signalled more to come — a genuinely unusual coordinated action that traders should respect, and one keeping a lid on the dollar broadly. In metals, the risk-on, growth-positive mix is doing the work — copper +1.2% and aluminium +1.8% on the strong US factory data, while gold softens and PGMs give back 2% as haven demand fades. Ags are running their own supply stories: cocoa exploded 10% higher with call buying out to 2027, sugar and the wheat complex firmed, and arabica pulled back 3% from records set on the US-Colombia tariff spat.

Watch Friday's payrolls and the $739 billion Treasury borrowing estimate — supply pressure on the long end hasn't gone away.

